WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.240
Speaker 1: We are in the middle of some pretty&nbsp;
historic times right now, and I don't say that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:10.560
in the meaningless sort of way, oh, it's&nbsp;
historic. The longest war for the United States&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:18.240
is ending and in some sense, it has ended&nbsp;
in the last few hours. Joe Biden announcing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:26.560
as well as Secretary of State Anthony Blinken,&nbsp;
that the United States has flown its final flight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:34.480
out of Afghanistan where the United States has&nbsp;
been for 20 years. There are now some images&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:42.400
that may become iconic or emblematic in some sense&nbsp;
as we go forward. The Associated Press reporting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:48.640
that the last troops have exited Afghanistan,&nbsp;
ending America's longest war. And there is this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:59.600
image this night, vision image of a military&nbsp;
service member provided to the Associated Press&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:08.560
boarding a C17 cargo plane at the airport in Kabul&nbsp;
yesterday, considered the final American service&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:14.400
member to depart Afghanistan. Now, in reality,&nbsp;
there's usually always some caveats, like there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:21.840
probably still a few there that are maybe in a&nbsp;
less official capacity or not in uniform or you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:26.720
never know. You guys understand what I'm saying.&nbsp;
But the point here is Joe Biden is officially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:31.440
getting the United States out, expected to address&nbsp;
the nation later today. I plan to cover it live&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:36.640
on YouTube, Twitch and Facebook. And the top&nbsp;
line here is that this is a major accomplishment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:43.360
The Associated Press writing, The US has completed&nbsp;
its withdrawal from Afghanistan, ending America's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:48.400
longest war and closing a chapter in military&nbsp;
history likely to be remembered for colossal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:54.240
failures, unfulfilled promises and a frantic&nbsp;
final exit that cost the lives of more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.240 --> 00:02:02.240
one hundred eighty Afghans and 13 U.S. service&nbsp;
members, some barely older than the war. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:09.680
there are many who want to argue that means that&nbsp;
it was wrong to leave. But it's really important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:18.000
to understand that if you go in in a harebrained,&nbsp;
ill thought out way, as George W. Bush did,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:26.320
and you remain for two decades as failures&nbsp;
continue to mount and you fail to achieve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:32.320
really almost anything other than the&nbsp;
temporary suppression of the Taliban,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:37.120
why would we assume getting out is going to&nbsp;
go swimmingly? In other words, it doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:42.960
follow logically if it was a disaster to get&nbsp;
in and it was a disaster while we were there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:48.400
is it a reasonable expectation to think that the&nbsp;
exit is going to be ginger snaps and Waterfall's?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:56.480
Now, I'm not being coy about the fact that&nbsp;
lives were lost in getting out in the same way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:01.200
that lives were lost while there. It's been some&nbsp;
time since American lives were lost while there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:09.040
But Afghan lives have continued to be lost there&nbsp;
and Afghan lives will likely continue to be lost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:16.400
there after our departure. But the top line&nbsp;
is that this is a major accomplishment in that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:23.280
the big arc is George W. Bush Gotterson.&nbsp;
It was a disaster. It was a quagmire.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:30.320
Barack Obama failed to get us out. Donald Trump&nbsp;
failed to get us out, and Joe Biden has gotten&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:36.320
us out. I'm not pretending that the rest is&nbsp;
irrelevant. It's not. It's people's lives. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:41.280
are things that could have been done better and&nbsp;
that applies to the entire time we were there and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:45.680
getting in in the first place. That's not unique&nbsp;
to Joe Biden. But the big picture is, I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:51.440
in 10 years and 20 years and 30 years, what is&nbsp;
the story of the war in Afghanistan? Obviously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:56.320
much of the story remains to be told. It'll depend&nbsp;
on what happens during that period of time. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.320 --> 00:04:04.240
the arc is George W. Bush got us in in 2001. We&nbsp;
stayed for 20 years. What the hell were we doing?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:11.600
And then Joe Biden got us out. That's the big&nbsp;
story. And it is not a story that is being told&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:18.640
in that clear terminology in too many places. Now,&nbsp;
there are some that will write in and say, David,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:23.120
it's not being told in that clear terminology&nbsp;
because it's not that clear and it's far more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:27.520
complicated. I'm with you. I'm not pretending&nbsp;
there isn't complexity here, but those are the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:33.680
big points. A little bit more from this Associated&nbsp;
Press article. Hours before President Joe Biden's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:39.200
Tuesday deadline for shutting down a final airlift&nbsp;
and thus ending the US war, Air Force transport&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:43.280
planes carried a remaining contingent of&nbsp;
troops from Kabul airport late Monday.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:47.520
Thousands of troops had spent a harrowing two&nbsp;
weeks protecting the airlift of tens of thousands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:52.960
of Afghans, Americans and others seeking to escape&nbsp;
a country once again ruled by Taliban militants.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:57.520
In announcing the completion of the evacuation and&nbsp;
war effort, Gen.. Frank Mackenzie, head of U.S.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:01.600
Central Command. And said the last planes took&nbsp;
off from Kabul Airport at three twenty nine p.m.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:06.960
Washington time or one minute before midnight&nbsp;
in Kabul, he said some American citizens likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:11.040
numbering in the very low hundreds were left&nbsp;
behind and he believes they will still be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:16.480
to leave the country. Secretary of State Anthony&nbsp;
Blinken put the number of Americans left behind at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:21.120
under two hundred, likely closer to one hundred,&nbsp;
and said the State Department would keep working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:26.480
to get them out. He praised the military led&nbsp;
evacuation as heroic and said the US diplomatic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:32.400
presence would shift to Doha, Qatar. Jake&nbsp;
Sullivan. Biden's NSA said Tuesday the mission&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:38.240
to get Americans out of Afghanistan continues.&nbsp;
Biden was set to address the nation on Afghanistan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:42.960
later Tuesday. We will cover that, as I mentioned,&nbsp;
Biden said in a written statement Monday. Military&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:47.440
commanders unanimously favored ending the airlift,&nbsp;
not extending it. He said he asked Blinken to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:52.960
coordinate with international partners in holding&nbsp;
the Taliban to their promise of safe passage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.960 --> 00:06:00.240
for Americans and others who want to leave in&nbsp;
the days ahead. This is a very important thing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:08.240
This is the right decision. This was an&nbsp;
unwinnable intervention. And the fact that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:15.520
listen, everything we've seen happen in these&nbsp;
two weeks proves that we shouldn't be there. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:23.440
Taliban so quickly taking over control, ISIS so&nbsp;
quickly getting in and doing a suicide bombing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:29.520
These are not reasons to stay. These are reasons&nbsp;
to leave. And it doesn't mean we don't care about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:35.680
the people there. But the U.S. simply can't be the&nbsp;
world's policeman in a situation that depends on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:41.120
our military presence in Afghanistan indefinitely&nbsp;
because of George W. Bush's hairbrained decision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:48.000
is untenable. And this is correct. I believe&nbsp;
history will bear that out with much tragedy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:52.240
In the meantime, there is no denying that&nbsp;
whatsoever. And what I want to talk about next is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:56.240
what's happening with Joe Biden's approval rating&nbsp;
partially because of Afghanistan, but mostly not

